            <ArticleTitle>Patient-performed pinnaplasty using industrial nail glue.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>75-77</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0165-5876(16)30456-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijporl.2016.12.020</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Malformations of the pinna occur in around 2-5% of all newborns. Prominent ears in childhood can be a source of psychological distress and lead to bullying and social exclusion. We present the case of a 14-year-old girl who inflicted chronic, non-healing wounds in the post auricular area bilaterally after attempting to glue back her ears with industrial strength glue. To our knowledge, this is the first case of its kind to be published.</AbstractText>
